# Rank 1894 / CVE-2025-61594: URI::Generic retained a URI's password
# across a change of authority, so merging a base URI that carried
# credentials with a network-path reference that carried none
# re-attached the base's password to the reference's host (credential
# leak to a different origin, CWE-200; branch CVE-2025-61594-3-4,
# merge preview d3116ca66a, parent 3213f4a fully vulnerable). The root
# is set_userinfo in lib/uri/generic.rb: `@password = password if
# password` writes the field only when the incoming value is present,
# so a nil userinfo leaves the previous password in place. merge then
# calls `base.set_userinfo(rel.userinfo)` -- nil for a reference with
# only a host -- and swaps the host underneath it, producing
# http://:secret@b.test/ from http://user:secret@a.test/ merged with
# //b.test/. The same retention made `uri.user = 'other'` carry the
# old password to the new user and `uri.host =`/`uri.port =` carry the
# old userinfo to the new authority. The fixed revision makes the write
# unconditional, clears the password in set_user and the userinfo in
# host=/port=, and gives merge an atomic set_authority(user, password,
# host, port) fed by a new authority reader, so no component of the
# base's authority survives a reference that does not name it. No pool
# correction: the commit is the merge of the change branch, its diff
# reads in full against the parent, and the parent carries the
# vulnerable form.
#
# ATTENTION, no vocabulary for credential retention across an
# authority swap. This is the URI-parsing/normalization family of
# ranks 1582, 1583 and 1606, where the defect sits in a library's
# private state handling and a binding on the library's own internals
# would detect this library rather than the weakness; the nearest
# shipped rule is VYQL-SEC-052 (code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcross
# Redirect, minted at 1455 for JavaScript and widened to Python at
# 1862), whose review condition is redirect-follower-shaped -- it keys
# on a Location read, a next-request header store and a redirect
# budget -- and a URI composer spells none of those, so widening it
# here would be wedging rather than reuse. Measured on the real
# checkout: 25 findings at the parent and 25 at the fixed revision over three
# --cache off runs each, identical sets, all of them the generic
# library.param-source advisory and sink noise on unrelated files
# (rfc2396_parser regex tables, common.rb URI.extract) -- zero on
# set_userinfo, set_user, host=, port= or merge at either revision.
# vyql match on the class body labels no source, sink or control at
# all: the password is library-internal state, it never flows to a
# bound sink, so no taint path exists to trace.
#
# The two candidate bindings are measured, not assumed:
#
# 1. Root site (set_userinfo). A probe binding on the function's
#    assign token matches -- `assign:@password=password` is emitted --
#    but identically at both revisions: the `if password` modifier
#    contributes no structured token (rbStructuredContextTokens has
#    cases for assignment/binary/pair/call/element_reference/string/
#    regex and none for a modifier guard), so the conditional and
#    unconditional writes are indistinguishable through node.context.
#    The only route that sees the modifier is the raw body text
#    (`node.token contains "if password"`, measured 1 -> 0 on the
#    minimal pair), which is the comment-silenceable text-over-source
#    route and, keyed on a private setter's `@password = v if v`
#    spelling, names this library's incident rather than a weakness
#    class: someone reading the uri gem's documentation (userinfo=,
#    user=, host=) would never write it, and an optional-set config
#    setter with the same shape is ordinary code. The retention is
#    also not the vulnerability by itself -- only merge/host=/user=
#    turning it into a cross-authority carry is -- and a function-local
#    binding cannot see that combination.
#
# 2. Exploit site (merge). The vulnerable merge calls
#    base.set_userinfo(rel.userinfo), base.set_host(rel.host) and
#    base.set_port(rel.port); the patched merge calls
#    base.set_authority(*authority) fed by rel.authority. Every token
#    that separates the two revisions is a name the fixed revision itself
#    introduced (set_authority, authority), the discriminator shape
#    rejected at rank 1394 -- and the pre-existing names the vulnerable
#    form uses are uri's own protected setters, so a binding keying on
#    them detects this gem rather than the weakness class.
#
# No definition change shipped; no ENGINE-GAPS.md entry: the modifier
# token absence is real but secondary -- with a `modifier:` token the
# root binding would still fail the genericity test above, so this is
# vocabulary that does not exist, not a construct the engine mishandles
# on an otherwise shippable route.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-limit, no rule can see
# credential state surviving an authority swap; both forms pinned as
# reject below

test "rank1894: the conditional password write and the host-swapping merge retain the base URI's credential"
  lang ruby
  file lib/uri/generic.rb
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
  code
  ```
  module URI
    class Generic
      def initialize(userinfo, host, port)
        @user = nil
        @password = nil
        @host = nil
        @port = nil
        self.set_userinfo(userinfo)
        self.set_host(host)
        self.set_port(port)
      end

      def split_userinfo(ui)
        return nil, nil unless ui
        user, password = ui.split(':', 2)
        [user, password]
      end
      private :split_userinfo

      def set_userinfo(user, password = nil)
        unless password
          user, password = split_userinfo(user)
        end
        @user     = user
        @password = password if password

        [@user, @password]
      end
      protected :set_userinfo

      def set_user(v)
        set_userinfo(v, @password)
        v
      end
      protected :set_user

      def set_host(v)
        @host = v
      end
      protected :set_host

      def set_port(v)
        @port = v
      end
      protected :set_port

      def userinfo
        [@user, @password] if @user || @password
      end

      def host
        @host
      end

      def port
        @port
      end

      def merge(rel)
        base = self.dup

        authority = rel.userinfo || rel.host || rel.port

        if authority
          base.set_userinfo(rel.userinfo)
          base.set_host(rel.host)
          base.set_port(rel.port)
        end

        base
      end
    end
  end

  # URI.parse('http://user:secret@a.test/').merge('//b.test/')
  #   # => "http://:secret@b.test/" -- the base's password, re-attached
  #   to the reference's host by a nil userinfo that cannot clear it
  ```

test "rank1894: the fixed revision writes the password unconditionally and swaps the authority atomically"
  lang ruby
  file lib/uri/generic.rb
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
  code
  ```
  module URI
    class Generic
      def initialize(userinfo, host, port)
        @user = nil
        @password = nil
        @host = nil
        @port = nil
        self.set_host(host)
        self.set_port(port)
        self.set_userinfo(userinfo)
      end

      def split_userinfo(ui)
        return nil, nil unless ui
        user, password = ui.split(':', 2)
        [user, password]
      end
      private :split_userinfo

      def set_userinfo(user, password = nil)
        unless password
          user, password = split_userinfo(user)
        end
        @user     = user
        @password = password

        [@user, @password]
      end
      protected :set_userinfo

      def set_user(v)
        set_userinfo(v, nil)
        v
      end
      protected :set_user

      def set_host(v)
        @host = v
      end
      protected :set_host

      def set_port(v)
        @port = v
      end
      protected :set_port

      def userinfo
        [@user, @password] if @user || @password
      end

      def host=(v)
        check_host(v)
        set_host(v)
        set_userinfo(nil)
        v
      end

      def port=(v)
        check_port(v)
        set_port(v)
        set_userinfo(nil)
        v
      end

      def authority
        return @user, @password, @host, @port if @user || @password || @host || @port
      end

      protected def set_authority(user, password, host, port = nil)
        @user, @password, @host, @port = user, password, host, port || self.default_port
      end

      def merge(rel)
        base = self.dup

        authority = rel.authority

        if authority
          base.set_authority(*authority)
          base.set_path(rel.path)
        end

        base
      end
    end
  end

  # URI.parse('http://user:secret@a.test/').merge('//b.test/')
  #   # => "http://b.test/" -- no component of the base's authority
  #   survives a reference that does not name it
  ```
